d on that  so what we did is we did a linear  regression where we looked at  bulk density by our dry weight of shoots  so shoots referring to everything from  the soil line up  so leaves and any woody biomass  that was developed and what we see is a  very strong relationship here of  in the blue we have our scoop and dump  we have  more shoots on the axis here as well as  higher bulk densities so what we see is  a very clear relationship where we're  actually looking at  um the lower our bulk density is  correlating very strongly with  more dry weights of shoots and we can  see in our unamended soil  that this line right here is our 1.4  threshold we start seeing root limiting  bulk densities  they're all kind of clustered down and  when we look  at the dry weight of roots as well we  see  a very similar pattern of our reduced  bulk density correlating with  more root growth  as well as our leaf area we see the same  pattern yet again  where we have strong correlations with  reductions in bulk density  and increases in leaf area so when we  did this  uh destructive harvest after we had um  pulled the plants out of the soils  containers that they had been growing in  uh there was a few observations that  were  they were made that weren't so easy to  necessarily quantify and one of the big  things that we saw was just  was just where the roots were exploring  in these containers  and how much more you know  biomass they're putting on so this is a  pretty clear picture of our scoop and  dump roots you can see  a lot more a much larger root system  developing here compared to our  unamended  which is just not nearly not nearly as  dense  and what was interesting when we looked  specifically  at where the roots were in the container  is that  in our unamended soils  plants that were growing in our soils  that had not been remediated  that had this higher bulk density what  we would typically see is that  where we planted the ficus in the  container there would be roots t