t is coming from if  what kind of source and how expensive  you can  that is going to be as well as the  machine use  but the one kind of point when we do  think about costs that i want to  bring up is just the idea  that we know that this technique  improves your soils over the long  term so you have to think about it more  in terms of  making an initial investment and then  over time  it's going to continue to pay off and  this is going to be particularly  pronounced if you're working in a  at a site that has had plant failure in  the past  by using this technique you're going to  hopefully be able to decrease on plant  failure  and therefore be saving money by not  having to replace those plants  nearly so often or hopefully not at all  and when it does come to selecting  plants to be putting in this bed  what we talk about in the urban  horticultural institute is always  selecting your plants based on your  site's limitation  and the urban horticult the urban  horticultural institute  has a number of different resources  available to help you figure out what  your site limitations are  and i'm just going to talk about them  kind of briefly here  so the first one we oftentimes consider  is to be cold hardiness  and most people are familiar with this  there's very good maps by the usda which  will tell you what zone you're in  and you always want to select your plant  first on in  based on zones so you know that it can  thrive on the  minimum temperature that you find in  your area  the next thing you consider is soil ph  and you're either going to have  you're going to have very you could have  very acidic soils  or very basic soils  and based on this again there's not much  we can do instead of select the right  plants that are going to  like that environment you can make  changes to soil ph that is  a possibility but oftentimes when we  think about in the urban setting  a cost that we do not tend to  incur because we don't actually tend to  change the soil's ph